# Testing rules

Never record a test as executed or passing unless it actually ran. Paste real output into
`docs/04-testing.md`, not expected output.

Prioritise high-defect-risk logic: authentication, authorization, business rules,
validation, matching, AI schema validation and fallback, and critical API paths. Do not
spend examination time testing trivial framework behaviour.

Name tests with the ID scheme so they trace back to requirements: `UT-`, `IT-`, `FT-`,
`ST-`, `UAT-`, `SEC-`, `PERF-`, `AI-TC-`. Reference the requirement ID the test covers in
the test description.

## Required coverage

**Auth** — valid login, invalid login, unauthorized access, protected resource access,
logout/session invalidation, role restriction.

**Authorization** (see `SEC-LG-*` in `docs/04-testing.md`) — a citizen cannot read another
citizen's intake; a lawyer cannot read an unassigned consultation; a citizen cannot call
admin endpoints; direct object IDs do not bypass ownership checks.

**AI** (see `AI-TC-*`) — clear employment issue, clear tenancy issue, ambiguous issue,
empty input rejected before the provider is called, provider failure falls back without
losing the submission, malformed provider output rejected by schema validation,
out-of-list category mapped to human review.

**Matching** — inactive or unapproved lawyers never appear; every recommendation reason
traces to configured criteria.

## Practice

Mock the AI provider in tests — never call the live provider from a test suite. Test the
fallback path as carefully as the happy path.

When fixing a defect: reproduce it, add or update a test, apply the smallest safe fix,
rerun related tests, check for regressions, and log it in `docs/04-testing.md` if it
matters to the report. Never mask an error to make a test pass, and never weaken an
assertion to get green.
